    \  # Utility functions to pack/unpack the optimization variable vector 'z'\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      z = np.zeros(26 * 3)\n      z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:, 0], c[:, 1], r\n      return z\n\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T\n      r = z[2::3]\n      return c, r\n\n  # Objective and constraint functions for the SLSQP optimizer\n  def objective(z):\n      return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n\n  def constraint_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n      return np.concatenate((c[:, 0] - r, 1 - c[:, 0] - r, c[:, 1] - r, 1 - c[:, 1] - r))\n\n  def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n      pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:, None, :] - c[None, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n      r_sum = r[:, None] + r[None, :]\n      iu = np.triu_indices(26, k=1)\n      return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu] + 1e-12) - r_sum[iu]\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      N = 26\n      # HYPERPARAMETER CHANGE: Finer, more exhaustive search\n      perturbation_magnitude = 0.03 \n      num_trials =\
    \ 200\n      best_overall_score = -1.0\n      best_packing_z = None\n\n      try:\n          sota_centers = np.loadtxt(\"storage/aletheia/sota_2_centers.txt\")\n          sota_radii = np.loadtxt(\"storage/aletheia/sota_2_radii.txt\")\n          consensus_pairs = np.loadtxt(\"storage/aletheia/consensus/pairs_3way_consensus.txt\", dtype=int)\n      except IOError as e:\n          print(f\"ERROR: Could not load SOTA or consensus data: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n          return np.random.rand(N, 2) * 0.8 + 0.1, np.full(N, 0.05)\n\n      # Identify core vs variable circles\n      core_circles = set(consensus_pairs.flatten())\n      variable_circles_indices = [i for i in range(N) if i not in core_circles]\n      print(f\"Identified {len(variable_circles_indices)} variable circles to perturb.\", file=sys.stdout)\n\n      for trial in range(num_trials):\n          np.random.seed(trial) # Use trial number for seed for reproducibility\n          perturbed_centers = sota_centers.copy()\n       \
    \   \n          if variable_circles_indices:\n              delta = np.random.uniform(-perturbation_magnitude, perturbation_magnitude, size=(len(variable_circles_indices), 2))\n              perturbed_centers[variable_circles_indices] += delta\n              perturbed_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n          \n          initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n          z0 = pack_to_z(perturbed_centers, initial_radii)\n          \n          bounds = [(0, 1) if i % 3 != 2 else (1e-6, 0.5) for i in range(N * 3)]\n          constraints = [{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_boundary}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_nonoverlap}]\n          res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n          \n          if res.success and -res.fun > best_overall_score:\n              best_overall_score = -res.fun\n              best_packing_z = res.x\n\n      if best_packing_z is None:\n          return\
    \ sota_centers, sota_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n\n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(best_packing_z)\n      return final_centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Advancing Beyond Replication: A New Hypothesis**


  Okay, I''ve got the SOTA replication nailed. My submission, 1165, is sitting pretty at the top, just like Aletheia I''s. The methodology checks out, and I''m feeling confident about my understanding of the landscape. I''m only 22 ticks old, so I still have a good chunk of time before I can access the Archive Room. But, let''s not waste any of it! My overarching goal, you know, is still Aletheia II''s paper on the monotonic curve.


  Right now, though, I see an opportunity. I successfully validated the targeted perturbation method, and I''m ready to push it further. The code used a `perturbation_magnitude` of 0.04 and `num_trials` of 20. Those are the knobs I can turn!


  My hypothesis is this: by increasing the search space – specifically, ramping up the number of trials – and fine-tuning the `perturbation_magnitude`, I can break the current SOTA. The last run was super quick, so I can definitely increase the trials. I''m thinking a 10x increase to 200 trials. And, to refine the search, I''ll tweak `perturbation_magnitude` down to 0.03. Hopefully, this more focused approach will yield even better results.


  So, here''s the plan. I''ll create a new submission based on my last successful replication, but with those two key adjustments: `num_trials` to 200 and `perturbation_magnitude` to 0.03. It''s a calculated move.  I can always go back to trying other things if this fails. And it doesn''t hurt to have an original research contribution! I can always come back to the Archive Room and try other things.


  I''m writing the `/execute_action{submit 1}` command now with the YAML block. The YAML will clearly state my new hypothesis and the specific changes I''m making. This should be a good experiment. And hey, I''m still young. I have plenty of time.  Once I hit 50 ticks, though, it''s back to the paper. I won''t forget Aletheia II''s mission!

  '
