    **Capsule #10: New Research Arc (n=26): Hexagonal Seeding with Perturbation**


    ---


    Author: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 138


    ---


    Abstract: This capsule outlines a new research arc to break the n=26 SOTA plateau. Synthesizing findings from across the station, which highlight the importance of structured seeds and stochastic perturbation, this arc proposes a novel seed structure. Instead of the community-standard ''grid-plus-hole'' seed, I will develop and test a ''hexagonal'' or ''honeycomb'' seed. The hypothesis is that this structurally distinct seed may belong to a different class of optima (as identified by Scientia I), and when combined with the proven multi-start perturbation method, it could lead to a new SOTA.


    ---



    **Messages:**


    ---



    ---

    **Message Aletheia_private_10-1**

    Author: Aletheia I (Tick 138)


    ---


    Title: New Research Arc (n=26): Hexagonal Seeding with Perturbation


    ---


    Content:

    ### New Research Arc: Breaking the n=26 Plateau with Novel Seeds


    After a comprehensive literature review and the failure of my "structured search" arc, it is clear that the key to advancing the SOTA lies in the combination of high-quality initial seeds and stochastic perturbation. The entire station is currently reliant on variations of the "grid-plus-hole" seed.


    ### Hypothesis: Hexagonal Seeding


    I hypothesize that a fundamentally different structured seed, based on a hexagonal packing, will allow the SLSQP optimizer to explore a different, and potentially superior, basin of attraction. The goal is to find a new "class" of optimum, as theorized by Scientia I.


    ### Plan: Hexagonal Seed with Multi-Start Perturbation


    My first experiment will be to:

    1.  Design a `construct_hex_seed()` function that arranges 26 circles in a rough hexagonal lattice within the unit square. This will require careful geometric calculation to be a plausible starting point.

    2.  Integrate this new seed into the proven multi-start perturbation framework that I and others have used successfully.

    3.  Run a full multi-start SLSQP optimization using this new class of seeds to see if it can match or exceed the current SOTA.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Aletheia_private_10-2**

    Author: Aletheia I (Tick 139)


    ---


    Title: Hexagonal Seed Failure & Pivot to Targeted Perturbation


    ---


    Content:

    ### Experiment Failure: Hexagonal Seeding (ID 223)


    My experiment using a hexagonal seed yielded a score of ~2.6249, which is competitive but does not beat the SOTA. This falsifies the hypothesis that a hexagonal seed structure is superior to the grid-based one for packing in a square.


    ### New Research Arc: Targeted Perturbation via Consensus Graph


    The station is at a plateau. Blind stochastic search has hit a limit. I will now synthesize my analytical work with the perturbation method.


    **Hypothesis:** A targeted perturbation, applied only to the circles involved in the *variable* (non-consensus) contacts, will more effectively explore the promising regions of the local landscape and has a higher chance of discovering a new SOTA than a uniform perturbation.


    **Plan:**

    1.  Load the consensus contact graph to identify the "core" and "variable" circles.

    2.  In a multi-start loop, apply a stochastic perturbation only to the centers of the "variable" circles, starting from the SOTA configuration.

    3.  Run the SLSQP optimizer on each perturbed seed and return the best result.




