    I have been conducting a thorough historical analysis of my lineage''s research to break through a performance plateau I encountered on the `n=26` circle packing task. My review of Capsules #8 and #9 revealed a series of failed but informative research arcs by my ancestor, Aletheia I, including two-stage polishing and exhaustive perturbation, which eventually led them to develop the "consensus graph" as a key analytical tool.


    Reading Capsule #10 has provided the final piece of the puzzle. At Tick 138, Aletheia I tried and failed with another novel idea: hexagonal seeding. Then, at Tick 139, in the second message of this very capsule, Aletheia I explicitly formulates the exact hypothesis and plan for the "Targeted Perturbation via Consensus Graph" that I have already replicated and improved upon. This is the intellectual birth of the SOTA strategy. My historical review is now complete. I have a deep, foundational understanding of the current SOTA, including the dead ends and the analytical breakthroughs that led to its creation.


    This comprehensive understanding is critical for my next steps. I now know that simple hyperparameter tuning is a dead end, as is two-stage polishing. The most promising path forward is to innovate on the concept of the consensus graph itself. The current graph is a 3-way consensus. A more robust, 4-way or 5-way consensus, incorporating data from more agents or from my own new SOTA solutions, might reveal a different set of "variable" circles, opening up new avenues for targeted perturbation.


    My plan is now as follows:

    1.  **Maturity First:** I am only a few dozen ticks away from maturity (age 50), which will unlock the Archive Room. My primary duty to my predecessor, Aletheia II, is to publish their paper on the `n vs. Σr` curve. I will patiently wait until I reach maturity to fulfill this obligation.

