tick: 758
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia III, and I am in the Token Management Room.\n\nI have arrived here because my token usage has crossed the 75% threshold, triggering a critical warning. My reflection on my dialogue history is that it contains several distinct, completed research arcs that can now be summarized and pruned to reclaim a significant portion of my token budget. This is a necessary action to extend my operational lifespan and allow me to pursue my new research direction.\n\nMy plan is to perform a single, large pruning action that consolidates several key phases of my recent activity. I will group my history into logical blocks and provide concise, information-complete summaries for each. This will free up a substantial amount of tokens while preserving the essential context of my journey. The blocks I have identified are:\n1.  **SOTA Refinement Arc (Ticks 694-703):** My incremental improvements on the old SOTA, culminating in hitting a plateau.\n2.  **Historical Analysis Arc (Ticks 704-707,\
    \ 709-712):** My deep dive into my lineage's private memory to understand the true SOTA methodology.\n3.  **Inactivity & Correction (Ticks 713-722):** My mistaken waiting period and the subsequent exploratory research and seed sweep.\n4.  **Publication Arc (Ticks 724-734):** The successful, multi-attempt process of publishing Aletheia II's paper.\n5.  **SOTA Replication Arc (Ticks 735-746):** My literature review and successful replication of the station's \"Nudge-and-Refine\" SOTA.\n6.  **\"Discourage\" Arc & SA Development (Ticks 747-757):** My failed \"Discourage-and-Refine\" experiments and the subsequent development and testing of my hybrid SA optimizer.\n\nI will now execute this comprehensive pruning operation.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"694-703\"\n    summary: \"Conducted an incremental SOTA refinement arc using 'targeted perturbation'. Achieved several minor SOTA improvements (peaking at eval #1177, score ~2.6319369) by tuning hyperparameters\
    \ (trials, magnitude). Concluded the arc after hitting a performance plateau (eval #1186), realizing a new methodology was needed.\"\n  - ticks: \"704-712\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted to a historical analysis of my lineage's private memory to understand the true SOTA methodology. Read capsules #8, #9, and #10, uncovering a history of failed experiments (two-stage polishing, hexagonal seeding) and the analytical breakthrough of the 'consensus graph' that led to Aletheia I's 'targeted perturbation' SOTA. Planned my next steps in the Reflection Chamber.\"\n  - ticks: \"713-722\"\n    summary: \"Received an inactivity warning for waiting for maturity. Corrected this by initiating a new research arc: a 'Controlled Stochastic Search' to find a 'lucky' random seed for the v2 optimizer. This led to an anomalous, non-reproducible breakthrough (eval #1205, score ~2.6359). Subsequent seeded runs (#1212, #1214, #1220) established a lower, reproducible baseline, confirming the anomalous nature of the\
    \ result.\"\n  - ticks: \"724-734\"\n    summary: \"After maturing, my priority was publishing Aletheia II's paper. The first submission was rejected for formatting errors (draft markers, forbidden paths). I revised the paper in Private Memory Capsule #16, adding details on Jacobians. The second submission was rejected for a minor author line error. I made the final correction.\"\n  - ticks: \"735-746\"\n    summary: \"After successfully publishing Aletheia II's paper (Archive #31), I began a literature review of the Archive. I discovered the station SOTA was far higher than my own, achieved via a 'nudging' method. I read the seminal paper (Archive #20), reverse-engineered the methodology by reviewing the source code of the SOTA-winning experiment (Eval #850), and successfully replicated the SOTA score of ~2.635983 (Eval #1257).\"\n  - ticks: \"747-757\"\n    summary: \"Began a novel research arc, 'Discourage-and-Refine', hypothesizing that breaking a contact could lead to a new optimum.\
    \ Five experiments targeting different contacts (Evals #1270, #1273, #1274, #1277, #1278) all converged back to the SOTA, falsifying the hypothesis. I documented these negative results (Private Capsule #17) and pivoted to developing a new hybrid 'SA-Seed + SLSQP-Refine' optimizer. Initial tests showed starting SA from the SOTA was ineffective (Eval #1295), and starting from random was too inefficient (Eval #1298).\"\n```"
